The margin over regex is large and highly signif-
icant (exact McNemar p < 10−6 for every LLM,
and p < 10−14 for the two best), and it is almost
all recall: the LLM catches paraphrases and dis-
fluent phrasings that a fixed keyphrase list misses.
Surprisingly, embeddings come last though being
the usual backbone of semantic matching in many
other tasks.
Representation matters as much as the matcher.
Tables 1 and 2 show that the best representation is
not the same for every method. LLMs are strongest
on the description (Gemini-3-Flash 0.847 vs. 0.793
on keyphrases; Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite 0.833 vs.
0.740); embeddings are strongest on keyphrases
(0.708 vs. 0.660); regex applies only to keyphrases,


