guage description, and how a topic is represented
is as consequential as which matcher scores it.
A lighter model is enough.
The two best sys-
tems are the two smallest LLMs, Gemini-3-Flash
and Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite, and no larger model
beats them. Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite is even nom-
inally ahead of the heavier Gemini-2.5-Pro and
Gemini-2.5-Flash (p = 0.014/0.019). This interest-
ing finding suggests that a small Flash-tier model
is enough for this task while heavier, most costly
LLMs offer no obvious benefits.
Cost and latency.
Table 3 prices the “lighter
is enough” finding.
Gemini-2.5-Pro is Pareto-
dominated: its mandatory thinking (∼525 output
tokens on a YES/NO question) makes it the slowest
(p50 5.6 s) and by far the costliest ($5.38 per 1,000
decisions), yet it does not lead on F1. Gemini-2.5-
Flash-Lite sits at the opposite corner: p50 0.35 s
and $0.010 per 1,000, i.e. ∼16× faster and ∼500×
cheaper than 2.5-Pro, at F1 within 0.02 of the best
matcher. Embeddings and regex are faster still and
self-hosted, but trail on F1 (Tables 1, 2). For a
latency- and cost-bounded real-time deployment,
a Flash-tier LLM on a description is the practical
operating point.
5.2
Across three matchers
Our central finding is that LLM matchers score sig-
nificantly higher on this noisy benchmark. What
is clear mechanistically is that regex matches only
literal keyphrases and cannot absorb disfluencies
or recognition errors, and that cosine similarity re-
wards surface overlap rather than topic semantics
